 of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $10,$ and the leading coefficient is $8.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }10}{\\text{factor of }8}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{-5}{4}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(-5)$ is a factor of $10$ and the denominator $(4)$ is a factor of $8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256900 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82ccfef3-569a-5ddf-9a69-cae323a0a312" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c67915b9-d3ad-5028-96ad-a45db46254f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269894} {:answer/id #uuid "2cb64211-4410-5663-bd84-281a8787bad5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2)/(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269895} {:answer/id #uuid "18d885f8-4fe3-5e0e-93d5-3e5dab645db5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269896} {:answer/id #uuid "fc481543-ca19-5317-a0ce-94d4f2e65c5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269897} {:answer/id #uuid "62f934d5-5b8c-5451-8c42-9e3f983c4321" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62f934d5-5b8c-5451-8c42-9e3f983c4321" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269898} :answer-field/id #uuid "97a1e573-580d-56e8-a91f-d31e3f8253c7" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "284a91b3-0aac-5ff1-be86-b1f45987e7b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-112871" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{7}-12{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}-3x+10?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $10,$ and the leading coefficient is $4.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }10}{\\text{factor of }4}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\frac{5}{4}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(5)$ is a factor of $10$ and the denominator $(4)$ is a factor of $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261882 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54623663-e1a9-5beb-8202-b574e377b574" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (4)/(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273863} {:answer/id #uuid "1e46b34f-f3e3-5c4d-b93d-47c76b5a1951" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273864} {:answer/id #uuid "6a713676-d0c3-5c15-8652-bf2984d13232" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273865} {:answer/id #uuid "a0cbea37-2764-5203-b2a1-0a26ba4cf015" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273866} {:answer/id #uuid "86886d58-d768-59d0-81b0-191e05e6e73f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273867}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a713676-d0c3-5c15-8652-bf2984d13232" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273865} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ad02e86-19dd-577c-b2dc-a3d95dc66c09" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a045cc6b-4cfa-5c4d-a695-16b0b2a1fa9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-83815" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+x-3?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-3$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-3}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $2$ does not take the above form. The value $2$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{2}{1},$ and the numerator $(2)$ is not a factor of $-3.$"}]}
